{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","count":17,"questions":[{"id":52562,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A bomb explodes at time $\\mathrm{t}=0$ in a uniform, isotropic medium of density $\\rho$ and releases energy E, generating a spherical blast wave. The radius $\\mathrm{R}$ of this blast wave varies with time t as :","options":["$\\mathrm{t}$","$t^{2 / 5}$","$t^{1 / 4}$","$t^{3 / 2}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"The energy will propagate in form of spherical blast wave which is longitudinal in nature. Hence velocity of propagate of disturbance\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FesCzqhC9tqNmMZIu-b_zhaiLzXLfigNf1pJ-XoYfLnM.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FesCzqhC9tqNmMZIu-b_zhaiLzXLfigNf1pJ-XoYfLnM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FesCzqhC9tqNmMZIu-b_zhaiLzXLfigNf1pJ-XoYfLnM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n\n\\mathrm{v}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma \\mathrm{P}}{\\mathrm{\\rho}}}\n\nWhere $P=$ Pressure\n\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\because \\quad \\mathrm{PV}=\\mathrm{nRT} \\\\\n\\mathrm{v}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma \\mathrm{RT}}{\\mathrm{V} \\rho}}\n\\end{array}\n\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\text { Where } V=\\frac{4 \\pi r^{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\& v=\\text { velocity of propagation }=\\frac{d r}{d t} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow \\frac{d r}{d t}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma R T}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}\\right) \\rho}} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{d r}{d t}=\\frac{k}{r^{3 / 2}} \\\\\n\\quad k=c o n s t a n t=\\sqrt{\\frac{3 \\gamma R T}{4 \\pi \\rho}} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow \\quad r^{3 / 2} d r=k d t \\\\\n\\qquad r^{3 / 2} d r=\\int k d t \\\\\nr^{5 / 2}=k t \\Rightarrow r=(k t)^{2 / 5} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow r \\propto t^{2 / 5}\n\\end{array}\n\nHence correct answer is (B)\nAlternate solution\n$\\mathrm{R} \\propto \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{P}^{\\mathrm{b}} \\mathrm{t}^{\\mathrm{c}}$\nDimension of $R=L\\begin{array}{c}\n\\mathrm{L} \\propto\\left(\\mathrm{ML}^{2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right)^{\\mathrm{a}}\\left(\\mathrm{ML}^{-3}\\right)^{\\mathrm{b}}(\\mathrm{T})^{\\mathrm{c}} \\\\\n2 \\mathrm{a}-3 \\mathrm{~b}=1 \\\\\n\\mathrm{c}-2 \\mathrm{a}=0 \\\\\n\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{b}=0\n\\end{array}\nfrom (i), (ii) \\& (iii)\n$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{a}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\\n\\mathrm{c}=2 \\mathrm{a} \\\\\n\\therefore \\quad \\mathrm{c}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n\\mathrm{R} \\propto \\mathrm{t}^{2 / 5}\n\\end{array}","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2137547c6051b0db6e4"},{"id":52563,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A hearing test is conducted on an aged person. It is found that her threshold of hearing is 20 decibels at $1 \\mathrm{kHz}$ and it rises linearly with frequency to 60 decibels at $9 \\mathrm{kHz}$. The minimum intensity of sound that the person can hear at $5 \\mathrm{kHz}$ is-","options":["10 times than that at $1 \\mathrm{kHz}$.","100 times than that at $1 \\mathrm{kHz}$","$0.5$ times than that at $9 \\mathrm{kHz}$","$0.05$ times than that at $9 \\mathrm{kHz}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F9bSs6FAR8MltREfeLUBFypizz3IjCH6NJUz3Zjv3f58.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F9bSs6FAR8MltREfeLUBFypizz3IjCH6NJUz3Zjv3f58.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F9bSs6FAR8MltREfeLUBFypizz3IjCH6NJUz3Zjv3f58.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nat $5 \\mathrm{KHz}$ Hearing capacity $=40 \\mathrm{~dB}$ Intensity at $1 \\mathrm{KHz}$\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\beta=10 \\log \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{I}}{\\mathrm{I}_{0}}\\right) \\\\\n\\mathrm{I}=\\mathrm{I}_{0} 10^{\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{10}\\right)} \\\\\n(\\mathrm{I})_{1 \\mathrm{KHz}}=\\mathrm{I}_{0} 10^{(20 / 10)}=\\mathrm{I}_{0}(10)^{2} \\\\\n(\\mathrm{I})_{5 \\mathrm{KHz}}=\\mathrm{I}_{0} 10^{(40 / 10)}=\\mathrm{I}_{0}(10)^{4} \\\\\n\\frac{(\\mathrm{I})_{1 \\mathrm{KHz}}}{\\mathrm{I}_{5 \\mathrm{KHz}}}=\\frac{1}{100}\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2134cacc84bafc56f9d"},{"id":52564,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A person in front of a mountain is beating a drum at the rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn></math> per minute and hears no distinct echo. If the person moves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi></math> closer to the mountain, he has to beat the drum at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn></math> per minute to not hear any distinct echo. The speed of sound is<br />\n ","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>320</mn><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>340</mn><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>360</mn><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>380</mn><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>As drummer does not hear any echo this means time between two successive wavefronts is equal to time in which a wavefront reaches back to drummer.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F341bf365-925c-48b6-9811-4cd27d6c5330-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F341bf365-925c-48b6-9811-4cd27d6c5330-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F341bf365-925c-48b6-9811-4cd27d6c5330-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>Distance covered by sound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi>x</mi></math></p>\n<p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo></math> speed of sound, then</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo></math> time interval between two successive</p>\n<p>wavefronts.</p>\n<p>So, we have</p>\n<p>In case I, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo> </mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>60</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac></math> ...(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></math>)</p>\n<p>In case II, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>60</mn><mn>60</mn></mfrac></math>  ... (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ii</mi></math>)</p>\n<p>Substituting for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> from Eq. (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></math>) in Eq. (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ii</mi></math>), we get</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></math></p>\n<p> </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></math>\n<p> </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>360</mn><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>\n<p> </p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da40a1878ed030bddb824"},{"id":52565,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The intensity of sound during the festival season increased by 100 times. This could imply a decibel level rise from","options":["20 to $120 \\mathrm{~dB}$","$70$ to $72 \\mathrm{~dB}$","100 to $10000 \\mathrm{~dB}$","80 to\n$100 \\mathrm{~dB}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Sound intensity $=10 \\log _{10}\\left(\\frac{I}{I_{0}}\\right)\\mathrm{I}=100 \\mathrm{I}_{0}$ so $10 \\times \\log \\frac{100 i_{0}}{i_{0}}=20$\nSo level rises by $20 \\mathrm{~dB}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21397197e1e8f074b30"},{"id":52566,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The clocktower (\"ghantaghar\") of Dehradun is famous for the sound of its bell, which can be heard, albeit faintly, upto the outskirts of the city <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi></math> away. Let the intensity of this faint sound be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo> </mo><mi>dB</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The clock is situated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> high. The intensity at the base of the tower is:-","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo> </mo><mi>dB</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>70</mn><mo> </mo><mi>dB</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mo> </mo><mi>dB</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo> </mo><mi>dB</mi></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>?</mo></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8000</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo> </mo><mi>dB</mi></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mfenced><mfrac><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mfenced></math><br />\nIntensity due to a point source, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mfenced><mfrac><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mfenced></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mfenced><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo> </mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>70</mn><mo> </mo><mi>dB</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7535d5193d5b32728f2"},{"id":52567,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A parallel beam of light is incident on a tank filled with water up to a height of $61.5 \\mathrm{~mm}$ as shown in the figure below. Ultrasonic waves of frequency $0.5 \\mathrm{MHz}$ are sent along the length of the water column using a transducer placed at the top, and they form longitudinal standing waves in the water. Which of the schematic plots below best describes the intensity distribution of the light as seen on the screen? Take the speed of sound in water to be $1,500 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s} .$\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FYt0HXX1CP7XXIijPHxkms0GFKwK0RGz7lxfHAGoFhio.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYt0HX