s given by </p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mi>μ</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>·</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>·</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>g</mi><mo>·</mo><mi>x</mi></msqrt></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></math></p>\n<p>Comparing above equation with </p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></math>, we get</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo></math> Acceleration, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>\n<p>Now, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo></math> time for wave pulse to cover a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>,</p>\n<p>then from </p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>,</p>\n<p>we have </p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n<p>or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mi>g</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt></math></p>\n<p>or time to travel complete length of rope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt></math>.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da720a302cc31fbd48688"},{"id":52569,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The figure below shows pressure variation in two different sound waves in air with time at a given position. Both the figures are drawn to the same scale.\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F0Y0HyQFy7Yl_QMlaojkWz0EneLdDU3nxvpTlSt3o0Tk.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F0Y0HyQFy7Yl_QMlaojkWz0EneLdDU3nxvpTlSt3o0Tk.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F0Y0HyQFy7Yl_QMlaojkWz0EneLdDU3nxvpTlSt3o0Tk.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nWhich of the following statements is true?","options":["Wave 1 has lower frequency and smaller amplitude compared to wave 2","Wave 1 has higher frequency and greater amplitude compared to wave 2","Wave 1 has shroter wavelength and greater amplitude compared to wave 2","Wave 1 has shorter wavelength and smaller amplitude compared to wave 2"],"answer":3,"solution":"Reason (brief):\n- The graphs are \\(P\\) vs \\(t\\) at a fixed position and same scale.\n- Wave 1 shows more oscillations in the same time \\(\\Rightarrow\\) higher frequency than Wave 2.\n- In the same medium, wave speed \\(v\\) is the same, so \\(v=f \\lambda \\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\frac{v}{f}\\). Hence higher \\(f\\) (Wave 1) \\(\\Rightarrow\\) smaller \\(\\lambda\\).\n- The peak displacement from the mean (pressure amplitude) is smaller for Wave 1 than for Wave 2. Therefore, Wave 1: shorter wavelength and smaller amplitude \\(\\rightarrow\\) option (4).","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213e92d06c94a9d7955"},{"id":52570,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A standing wave in a pipe with a length $L=1.2 \\mathrm{~m}$ is described by\n$y(x, t)=y_{0} \\sin [(2 \\pi / L) x] \\sin [(2 \\pi / \\mathrm{L}) x+\\pi / 4]$\nBased on above information, which one of the following statements is incorrect. (Speed of sound in air is $300 \\mathrm{~m} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ )-","options":["The pipe is closed at hoth ends","The wavelength of the wave could be $1.2 \\mathrm{~m}$","There could be a node at $\\mathrm{x}=0$ and antinode at $\\mathrm{x}=\\mathrm{L} / 2$","The frequency of the fundamental mode of vibrations is $137.5 \\mathrm{~Hz}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda} \\mathrm{x}=\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\mathrm{L}} \\mathrm{x} \\\\\n\\therefore \\lambda=\\mathrm{L}=1.2 \\mathrm{~m} \\\\\n\\text { at } \\mathrm{x}=0, \\mathrm{x}=\\mathrm{L}, \\mathrm{y}=0 \\\\\n\\mathrm{v}=\\frac{\\mathrm{v}}{\\lambda}=\\frac{300}{1.2}=250 \\mathrm{~Hz}\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2134b03fd64e7ec7fb2"},{"id":52571,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A speaker emits a sound wave of frequency $f_{0}$. When it moves towards a stationary observer with speed $u$, the observer measures a frequency $f_{1}$. If the speaker is stationary, and the observer moves towards it with speed $\\mathrm{u}$, the measured frequency is $f_{2}$. Then -","options":["$f_{1}=f_{2} \\lt  f_{0}$","$f_{1}\\gt f_{2}$","$f_{1} \\lt  f_{2}$","$f_{1}=f_{2}\\gt f_{0}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FmvJgrmGKJLbNNPpgDAX60NgSBEdoc3ganVuQONUWPxg.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FmvJgrmGKJLbNNPpgDAX60NgSBEdoc3ganVuQONUWPxg.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FmvJgrmGKJLbNNPpgDAX60NgSBEdoc3ganVuQONUWPxg.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\mathrm{f}_{1}=\\frac{\\mathrm{f}_{0}[\\mathrm{v}]}{\\mathrm{v}-\\mathrm{u}}$<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FiQCNg7NjzPKzbrCLAPnQgQcL4dK4YSyymSdFWZO2P9M.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FiQCNg7NjzPKzbrCLAPnQgQcL4dK4YSyymSdFWZO2P9M.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FiQCNg7NjzPKzbrCLAPnQgQcL4dK4YSyymSdFWZO2P9M.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$f_{2}=f_{0} \\frac{[v+u]}{v}f_{2}-f_{1}=f_{0}\\left(\\frac{u+v}{v}-\\frac{v}{v-u}\\right)f_{2}-f_{1}=\\frac{-u^{2} f_{0}}{(v)(v-u)}=-v e\\therefore f_{1}\\gt f_{2}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213ad806eb37773719a"},{"id":52572,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A bus driving along at $39.6 \\mathrm{kmph}$ is approaching a person who is standing at the bus stop, while honking repeatedly at an interval of 30 seconds. If the speed of the sound is $330 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}$, at what interval will the person hear the horn?","options":["31 seconds","29 seconds","30 seconds","the interval will depend on the distance of the bus from the passenger"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fx7bWc1sIbcgks1DO_Lao49boSVfYWGlAkWfHhWyUER8.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fx7bWc1sIbcgks1DO_Lao49boSVfYWGlAkWfHhWyUER8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fx7bWc1sIbcgks1DO_Lao49boSVfYWGlAkWfHhWyUER8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\mathrm{v}=39.6 \\mathrm{~km} / \\mathrm{hr}=11 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}, \\quad \\mathrm{t}_{1}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{330}$ and $\\mathrm{t}_{2}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}-11 \\times 30}{330}$\nNow $\\Delta t=t_{1}-t_{2}=1$\nnow $t_{1}=30 \\mathrm{sec} \\therefore t_{2}=29 \\mathrm{sec}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213eeab8f3e1e00e569"},{"id":52573,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"At $23^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, a pipe open at both ends resonates at a frequency of 450 hertz. At what frequency does the same pipe resonate on a hot day when the speed of sound is 4 percent higher than it would be at $23^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ ?","options":["$446 \\mathrm{~Hz}$","$454 \\mathrm{~Hz}$","$468 \\mathrm{~Hz}$","$459 \\mathrm{~Hz}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{f} \\lambda=\\mathrm{v}\\mathrm{f} \\propto \\mathrm{v}\\frac{\\mathrm{f}_{1}}{\\mathrm{f}_{2}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{v}_{1}}{\\mathrm{v}_{2}}\\frac{450}{\\mathrm{f}_{2}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{v}_{1}}{1.04 \\mathrm{v}_{1}}\\mathrm{f}_{2}=1.04 \\times 450=468 \\mathrm{~Hz}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213325a685e76ddc4a5"},{"id":52574,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>Two identical coherent sound sources <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math> with frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> apart. An observer standing equidistant from the source and at a perpendicular distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> from the line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> hears maximum sound intensity.</p>\n\n<p>When he moves parallel to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math>, the sound intensity varies and is a minimum when he comes directly in front of one of the two sources. Then, a possible value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi></math> is close to (the speed of sound is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>330</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>)</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>495</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>275</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>660</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>330</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F9ebabd8d-2b97-4260-bec9-97e5e516dca0-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F9ebabd8d-2b97-4260-bec9-97e5e516dca0-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F9ebabd8d-2b97-4260-bec9-97e5e516dca0-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>For a minima at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>, path difference of sounds reaching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> must be an odd multiple of half wavelength. </p>\n<p>So, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p>\n<p>where, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>…</mo></math></p>\n<p>From above figure,</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mn>12</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math></p>\n<p>So, path difference, </p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math></p>\n<p>Hence, from Eq. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>)</mo></math>,</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>f</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n<p>or frequency of sound, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mi>v</mi></math></p>\n<p>Possible values of frequency of sound are<br/>\nfor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, </p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>330</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>165</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math></p>\n<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>,</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>495</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo></math> etc.</p>\n<p>Hence, option '<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math>' matches with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7166cfa7e4f2778156a"},{"id":52575,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Waves and Sound","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A closed pipe of length $300 \\mathrm{~cm}$ contains some sand. A speaker is connected at one of its ends. The frequency of the speaker at which the sand will arrange itself in 20 equidistant piles is close to (velocity of sound is $300 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathr